            <ArticleTitle>Is a history of gestational diabetes related to risk factors for coronary heart disease?</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>298-306</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1002/nur.20325</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Coronary heart disease (CHD) risk in 20 non-diabetic women with and 20 without a distant history of gestational diabetes (hGDM), matched on age, body mass index, and time since GDM-affected pregnancy, was compared in a case control study. Women with an hGDM had lower high-density lipoprotein cholesterol (HDL-c), p = .02, and higher triglycerides, p &lt; or = .001, versus controls. The combination of high triglycerides and low HDL-c occurred in 25% of hGDM cases versus 0% of controls, p &lt;/= .01. Two-hour post-load glucose indicated that 45% of hGDM cases were pre-diabetic versus 20% of controls, p &lt; or = .05. Non-diabetic women with a distant hGDM are at increased risk for DM and may be at increased risk for CHD.</AbstractText>
                <CopyrightInformation>(c) 2009 Wiley Periodicals, Inc.</CopyrightInformation>
            </Abstract>
